WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.466
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:09.234
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:16.389
Unit thirty-seven: Discourse Markers in Speech. Today you will manage conversation with natural fillers and signals.

4
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.272
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.338
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:24.685
Well.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:28.588
Well means introduces a response or hesitation.

8
00:00:29.183 --> 00:00:30.730
Well, I am not sure.

9
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:33.354
Your turn. Say: Well.

10
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.131
Well.

11
00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:38.768
I mean.

12
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:42.178
I mean means clarifies or corrects.

13
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:44.818
I mean, it is not bad.

14
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.732
Your turn. Say: I mean.

15
00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:51.521
I mean.

16
00:00:52.283 --> 00:00:53.273
Anyway.

17
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:56.770
Anyway means returns to the main topic.

18
00:00:57.383 --> 00:00:59.116
Anyway, back to the plan.

19
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:02.369
Your turn. Say: Anyway.

20
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:06.025
Anyway.

21
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:07.645
You know.

22
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.719
You know means checks shared knowledge.

23
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:13.072
It was, you know, difficult.

24
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:15.686
Your turn. Say: You know.

25
00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:19.442
You know.

26
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:21.173
Actually.

27
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:25.147
Actually means introduces a surprising fact.

28
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.274
Actually, I disagree.

29
00:01:27.883 --> 00:01:30.605
Your turn. Say: Actually.

30
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:34.503
Actually.

31
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.646
As a matter of fact.

32
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.728
As a matter of fact means adds a true, often surprising point.

33
00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:44.397
As a matter of fact, I live there.

34
00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:48.088
Your turn. Say: As a matter of fact.

35
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:52.468
As a matter of fact.

36
00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:54.345
To be honest.

37
00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.000
To be honest means introduces a frank opinion.

38
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:00.269
To be honest, I was bored.

39
00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:03.930
Your turn. Say: To be honest.

40
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:07.884
To be honest.

41
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:09.802
By the way.

42
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:13.536
By the way means introduces a new, side topic.

43
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:15.952
By the way, did you call her?

44
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.889
Your turn. Say: By the way.

45
00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:22.781
By the way.

46
00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:27.484
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.660
Visal, why do native speakers say Well, I mean, you know so often?

48
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:46.178
They are discourse markers. They do not add meaning to the facts, but they organise the conversation, show hesitation, soften opinions and keep the other person involved.

49
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:48.331
What do they do?

50
00:02:49.417 --> 00:03:04.221
Well introduces a cautious answer. I mean clarifies. Anyway returns to the main topic. By the way adds a side topic. To be honest and actually introduce frank or surprising points.

51
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:06.887
Are they all acceptable?

52
00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:24.117
In speech yes, but overuse of you know sounds careless. In formal writing, avoid most of them. Choose a marker that fits the situation, and place it at the start or end of an utterance with a short pause.

53
00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.697
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:33.416
Grammar focus: Discourse markers: marker plus comma plus clause (speech).

55
00:03:33.716 --> 00:03:34.868
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.583 --> 00:03:37.792
Well, I am not sure about that.

57
00:03:38.683 --> 00:03:41.054
To be honest, I found it a little boring.

58
00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.112
By the way, did you call her yesterday?

59
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:53.359
Discourse markers organise speech: well (hesitation), I mean (clarify), anyway (return), by the way (side topic), actually (surprise).

60
00:03:54.117 --> 00:03:58.209
Be careful! Don't say: Actually, it is a good idea, you know, anyway.

61
00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:02.851
Say: Actually, it is a good idea.

62
00:04:03.151 --> 00:04:04.710
Do not overuse markers.

63
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:08.210
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.094
Just Talking.

65
00:04:10.394 --> 00:04:13.020
Well, I am not sure that is a good idea.

66
00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:16.880
I mean, it could work if we planned carefully.

67
00:04:17.867 --> 00:04:20.447
Anyway, let's go back to the schedule.

68
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:24.071
Actually, I think the first plan was better.

69
00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:27.322
By the way, did you send the invitation?

70
00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:32.331
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:33.183 --> 00:04:36.463
blank, I am not sure about that. (hesitation)

72
00:04:36.763 --> 00:04:40.161
A, Well. B, Anyway. C, Actually.

73
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.572
The answer is A: Well.

74
00:04:46.717 --> 00:04:48.832
Which returns to the main topic?

75
00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:52.612
A, well. B, anyway. C, by the way.

76
00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:58.306
The answer is B: anyway.

77
00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.589
Which introduces a side topic?

78
00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:05.786
A, by the way. B, anyway. C, to be honest.

79
00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:11.275
The answer is A: by the way.

80
00:05:12.417 --> 00:05:15.008
Discourse markers are mostly used in blank.

81
00:05:15.308 --> 00:05:19.519
A, dictionaries. B, speech. C, legal contracts.

82
00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:24.973
The answer is B: speech.

83
00:05:26.117 --> 00:05:28.476
According to the reading, what should be sent?

84
00:05:28.776 --> 00:05:32.929
A, the invitation. B, the schedule. C, the report.

85
00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:38.606
The answer is A: the invitation.

86
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.952
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:43.817 --> 00:05:47.186
Write a short dialogue using three discourse markers.

88
00:05:55.983 --> 00:05:57.518
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:57.818 --> 00:06:14.306
A: Well, what did you think of the film? B: To be honest, I found it slow. A: Actually, I loved it! B: Anyway, shall we get dinner? The markers make the dialogue sound natural.

90
00:06:14.606 --> 00:06:16.083
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:16.983 --> 00:06:23.583
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and discourse markers: marker plus comma plus clause (speech).

92
00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:28.219
Today's value is authentic living. Live by choices that are truly your own.

93
00:06:28.519 --> 00:06:31.252
See you in Unit thirty-eight. Goodbye!

94
00:06:31.552 --> 00:06:34.959
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

